WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.200
What do Americans think about immigration
and particularly the Dreamers?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.440
In polls conducted since the 1960s, only small
numbers of Americans have ever wanted to increase

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.440
immigration.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.269
Only 21 percent gave that response in 2016
when Gallup last asked the question.

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:24.820
Thirty eight percent wanted to keep immigration
at is present level and an identical 38 percent

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.140
wanted to decrease it.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.960
Forty-three percent in a new Monmouth University poll say illegal immigration

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.480
is a serious problem for the United States.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.840
But immigration attitudes are complex.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.969
Recent polls show that Americans are becoming
more supportive of the contributions immigrants

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:40.489
make to our society.

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:45.149
And most people favor a path to citizenship
for law-abiding illegal aliens.

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:49.699
Americans are particularly sympathetic to
young people who were brought here as children

00:00:49.699 --> 00:00:51.309
by parents who are illegal.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:56.019
Majorities support the DACA program that gives
these young people temporary legal status,

00:00:56.019 --> 00:01:00.430
and President Trump’s decision to end the
program is not popular, even among some Republicans.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:05.240
The Monmouth poll shows that 70 percent nationally
would allow these young people to become US

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.780
citizens if they don’t have criminal records.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.500
